\begin{abstract}
  \whyse (WEB Hypertext System in Emacs) is an integrated development
  environment for noweb-based hypertexts within Emacs. While named for
  WEB, it does not support that nor CWEB; the name is primarily to
  reference the inspiration. It is motivated by, and based on, a
  conference proceeding written in \cite[1991 by Brown and
  Czejdo]{brown1991}
\end{abstract}





\part{\whyse{} and its implementation}
\label{part:what-is-whyse}





\chapter{Preface}\label{Preface}
The RDBMS architecture proposed by Brown \& Czejdo isn't implemented in
this version of \whyse{}, but for completeness is planned. The main
features implemented are indices for chunks and identifiers, in the
style of the \reftex{} table of contents as well as other views, a graph
view of chunks and identifiers, and a VC-based project-oriented workflow
used as the backend of these features (the function \texttt{vc-root-dir}
and some features of customize are relied upon).

Users of \whyse{} are expected to be familiar with noweb. Users must
know how to write their own pipelines rather than only using
\texttt{noweave} and \texttt{notangle} (the noweb manual explaining the
\texttt{-v} option is a starting point for those not familiar enough
with noweb). Knowing how to write filters isn't required.

To use either noweb or \whyse{} to their fullest, users should read and
understand the \textit{noweb Hacker's Guide}. Reading the implementation
of \whyse{} afterwards is recommended; reading the implementation of
peg-noweb isn't required unless you're a serious hacker.

Modifying \whyse{} without using \whyse{} would be counter-intuitive,
yet effort is made to produce readable output code and structured,
readable webs. Nevertheless, unless something is broken you should try
to leverage \whyse{} to improve itself.

The implementation of the whyse \textit{package} begins with the indices
feature.





\chapter{Indices}
\whs described several indices used for navigation between modules.
These indices are the core idea implemented in \whyse, whereas the
\sql architecture and windowing are deliberately avoided. The indices
specified in \whs were,
\begin{itemize}
\item identifier index
\item topical index
\item module index
\item section index
\end{itemize}
whereas I only plan to implement the identifier and module indices in
the first release of \whyse. The topical index depends upon specific
behaviour of the traditional WEB. The section index is already suitably,
and better-implemented, by RefTeX. Eventually, extension of RefTeX and
AUCTeX is the goal, rather than providing a wholly different interface.

\section{Module index}
% TODO: whyse--make-index-modules
The module index provides a tabular interface to the references to other
modules made by each module. The interface is provided by the
\texttt{ctable} package, which requires tabular data be provided in a
list of rows, so the parse tree of a noweb document is first gathered.

The \latex command \texttt{\nowebchunks} emits the chunk index, which is
facilitated by the \texttt{-index} command of noweave.

<<example noweave pipeline with autodefs and indexing>>=
markup whyse.nw | ./autodefs.elisp | finduses | noidx -delay > /tmp/whyse.tool
@ 

The information necessary to create a hyperlink back to the source text from
the index is generated already, so all that needs to be done is to
transform it into [[:data]] and metadata usable by ctable hook functions.

<<the [[whyse-open-project-functions]] abnormal hook and its default functions>>=
(defun whyse--make-index-modules ()
  "Create the *whyse-modules* buffer and show it in a window."
  (let ((modbuf (get-buffer-create "*whyse-modules*")))
    (with-current-buffer modbuf
      (insert parse-tree); FIXME: this probably won't work.
      (split-window)
      (switch-to-buffer-other-window modbuf))))
@ 

