\chapter{\whyse{} Projects}\label{Projects}
A user will read from \textit{the package description} that they should
call an interactive command to create a project. The \whyse{}
application is started with the interactive command: [[whyse]]. The
command loads the first element of the customization variable
[[whyse-registered-projects]] (a list), considering that the default
project, or it opens the ``Easy Customization Interface'' for the
application's customization group ([[M-x~customize-group~whyse]]): an
effective prompt for the user to enter the necessary information. If
user's dislike this, they can disable it.

<<Customization and global variables>>=
(defgroup whyse nil
  "WEB Hypertext System in Emacs"
  :tag "WHYSE"
  :group 'applications)

(defcustom whyse-registered-projects nil
  "This variable stores all of the projects that are known to WHYSE."
  :group 'whyse
  :type '(repeat whyse--project-widget)
  :require 'widget
  :tag "WHYSE Registered Projects")

@

%% NOTE: The Widget feature is required by the registered projects
%% variable, but may be redundant because the Easy Customization
%% Interface is itself implemented with The Emacs Widget Library.
%% Requiring the library may be undesirable, as (require 'widget) will
%% be eagerly evaluated upon Emacs' initialization when
%% whyse-registered-projects is set to its saved custom value. However,
%% there may be a good reason to eagerly evaluate that form: the Widget
%% feature will be available immediately, and widgets will be used in
%% buffers to provide TUI buttons for navigation between modules of a
%% literate program (at least, that is the design of the program at this
%% point in development), so having this feature available sooner than
%% later is okay. The feature is required by the package regardless.

The [[whyse--project-widget]] type used for the registered projects variable
is a simple list widget containing the name of the project and a path to
its noweb source file, along with a shell command which generates the
noweb tool syntax for this project. Each noweb project has a different
command-line, and some are complex enough to have a makefile, or
multiple makefiles! noweb itself is an example of that level of
complexity. The shell command is executed by \whyse{} upon loading the
project, and the standard output captured for parsing by a PEG parser
defined in the [[peg-noweb]] utility package.

The widget itself is explained in \ref{widgetry}.

\section{Widgetry}\label{widgetry}
<<Widgets>>=
(define-widget 'whyse--project-widget 'list
  "The WHYSE project widget type."
  :format "\n%v\n"
  :offset 0
  :indent 0

  ;; NOTE: the convert-widget keyword with the argument
  ;; 'widget-types-convert-widget is absolutely necessary for ARGS to be
  ;; converted to widgets.
  :convert-widget 'widget-types-convert-widget
  :args '((editable-field
           :format "%t: %v"
           :tag "Name"
           :value "")

          (file
           :tag "noweb source file (*.nw)"
           :format "%t: %v"
           :valid-regexp ".*\\.nw$"
           :value "")

          (string
           :tag "A shell command to run a shell script to generates noweb tool syntax"
           :format "%t: %v"
           :documentation "A shell script which will produce the
           noweb tool syntax. Any shell commands involved with
           noweave should be included, but totex should of course
           be excluded from this script. The script should output
           the full syntax to standard output. See the noweb
           implementation of WHYSE for explanation."
           :value "")))

@

An example of what the list generated from the information entered into
Customize would look like is given here for elucidation (as it would
exist in a [[custom-set-variables]] form).

\begin{verbatim}
'(whyse-registered-projects
  '(("WEB Hypertext System in Emacs"
     "~/src/whyse/src/whyse.nw"
     "make -C "~/src/whyse/build" --silent --file ~/src/whyse/Makefile tool-syntax"))
  nil
  (widget))
\end{verbatim}





\section{Commands}
At the moment there is only one command, [[<<[[whyse]]>>]]. This
command, effectively, handles project registration and in subsequent
calls will run all hooks when it ``opens a project''. Opening a project
either finds the file for a known project and opens the module index, or
it just opens the module index if the user is already editing a file
within the project.

<<[[whyse]]>>=
(defun whyse ()
  "Opens the default whyse project, conditionally running hooks.

In effect, open the main file for the project and run hooks to prepare
the indices. Hooks are only run if a project is actually opened; if both
`whyse-load-default-project?' and
`whyse-open-customize-when-no-project-defined?' are nil then a warning
is given and the `whyse-open-project-functions' is not run.

When both customization variables are non-nil, or if only
`whyse-load-default-project?' is nil, then Customize is opened to the
whyse group."
  (interactive)
  ;; Warn the user that their customization options have made `whyse' a
  ;; no-op function.
  (when (and (not whyse-load-default-project?)
             (not whyse-open-customize-when-no-projects-defined?))
    (warn "The current customization options in the `whyse' group have effectively disabled the `whyse' command."))
  (if-let* ((whyse-load-default-project?)
            (default-project (cl-first whyse-registered-projects))
            (project (list :name (cl-first default-project)
                           :noweb (cl-second default-project)
                           :script (cl-third default-project))))
      (progn (if whyse-open-project-frame; open in a frame or the current window.
                 (if (eq t whyse-open-project-frame)
                     (make-frame); else make a frame with the parameters stored in the flag.
                   (make-frame whyse-open-project-frame))
               ;; Open the project in the appropriate buffer. TODO: watch
               ;; Prot's video about buffer management to get a sense of how
               ;; applications should behave by default. Which buffer should I
               ;; open in? The current buffer? Anot her buffer?
               (find-file (plist-get project :noweb)))
             (run-hooks-with-args 'whyse-open-project-functions (current-buffer) project))
    (if whyse-open-customize-when-no-projects-defined?
        (customize-group 'whyse)
      (warn "No `whyse' projects are defined! Call `customize-group' with `whyse' to setup a project."))))

@

Opening a project means the indices will need to be available, so the
project script will be ran to produce the tool syntax needed to generate
the indices for navigation.

