<<Customization and global variables>>=
(defcustom whyse-load-default-project? t
  "Non-nil values mean the system will load the default project.

nil will cause the interactive command `whyse' to open Customize on
its group of variables."
  :type 'boolean
  :group 'whyse
  :tag "Load default project when `whyse' is invoked?")

(defcustom whyse-open-customize-when-no-projects-defined? t
  "Non-nil values mean the system will open Customize as necessary.

nil will cause `whyse' to simply do nothing when no project is
defined."
  :type 'boolean
  :group 'whyse
  :tag "Open Customize to the whyse group when `whyse' is invoked and no projects are defined?")

(defcustom whyse-open-project-frame t
  "Non-nil means to open a new frame in which to edit a whyse project.

The value is any additional frame parameters or `t'."
  :type '(choice (or boolean list))
  :group 'whyse
  :tag "A list of frame parameters to use when opening a whyse project in a new frame, or `nil' to disable opening projects in new frames.")

@





\section{Customizing the behaviour of \whyse{} with hooks}
\whyse{} is meant to be customizable, defining as little as necessary to
implement a development environment for noweb inspired by
\cite{brown1991}. The default behaviour of \whyse{} is to do nothing
after opening a project, so no default hook functions are defined.

<<the [[whyse-open-project-functions]] abnormal hook and its default functions>>=
(defvar whyse-open-project-functions '()
  "Functions to call when `whyse' has opened a project.

Each function must take at least one argument, the whyse project plist.")

(defun whyse--make-index (project &optional modules?)
  "Create the *nw index* buffer.

When MODULES? is non-nil the module index will be displayed in the
bufer, otherwise the identifier index is displayed."
  (if (eq t modules?) (whyse--make-index-modules project)
    (whyse--make-index-identifiers project)))

(add-hook 'whyse-open-project-functions #'whyse--make-index)
@





\part{Appendices} \label{part:appendices} \appendix
\chapter{Packaging \whyse{}}
\whyse{} follows the form of single-file packages documented in the
\textit{Emacs Lisp Reference Manual}. The package file, \whyseel{}, is
emitted by \texttt{notangle} which is rebuilt by Make in every
target but [[clean]].
The makefile distributed alongside \whysenw{} in the tarball contains the
command-line used to tangle and weave \whyse{}.

